 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
 Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+The concurrency policy (whole-visit revisions) defines the submission metadata and retrieval representation below.
+
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Decision**: Each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs and displays pending competing revisions. Review-export needs a revision comparison workflow and excludes unresolved visits from its export set.
+
+**Rationale**: A bigger review queue is preferred over maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. The team accepts higher coordinator workload to avoid silent semantic combination.
+
+### Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Whole-visit preserves each worker's complete intent as submitted. Field-aware merge reduces review volume but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries — and automatically combined fields can cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand (e.g., changing a species ID without adjusting its cover estimate, or adding a species that conflicts with an existing one). The team prefers visible review work over invisible semantic combination. |
+| Revision numbering | Sequential integers per visit | UUIDs, timestamps | Sequential integers are compact, human-readable in review, and make "base_revision" equality checks trivial. |
+| Head selection among competitors | Highest revision number (most recent arrival) | Coordinator picks during resolution | Arrival order carries no authority signal; the head is simply the latest arrival until a coordinator resolves. This keeps the server policy neutral. |
+| Resolution creates new revision | Yes (head + 1) | Overwrite head in place | Creating a new revision preserves the full history including the resolution itself, and keeps the revision chain linear. |
+| Unresolved visits in export | Excluded | Included with flag | The partner receives a reviewed CSV. Unresolved visits have no single authoritative state to export. Excluding them keeps the export contract clean. |
+
+## Submission Envelope
+
+A submission carries:
+- `visit_id` (UUID) — the visit being edited
+- `base_revision` (integer) — the revision number the edit started from
+- `fields` (object) — the complete visit fields: `observation_date`, `observers` (string[]), `species` (array of `{species_id, cover_percent}`), `notes` (string)
+- `idempotency_key` (string) — client-generated, unique per submission attempt
+
+The server accepts a submission if:
+- The `visit_id` exists (or is new)
+- The `base_revision` matches the current head revision of that visit
+- The `idempotency_key` is unused (or matches an identical prior payload)
+
+On acceptance, the server assigns the next revision number (`base_revision + 1`), stores the submission as a new revision, and returns the assigned revision number.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
